 or to make someone stop  believing in the idea of romantic love  very uh  problematic situation that that many of  us find ourselves in  where we say  i don't like the way i'm feeling  i don't like what's happened i don't  like what my role in it was i don't like  where this is going but i can't you know  stamp my fingers and stop feeling  this way  so  he's asking what what are the conditions  that make it so that this is the case  and i don't think that you can say that  there is one single general schema that  you can put all of this in  you you do have to look at this in a  way that focuses on the person and the  relationships that they're in and their  understandings  of relationships and of themselves  that's not to say that it's like totally  you know up for grabs none of us can  compare it you know to each other where  all these  per you know  little monads or as we now call it  unique snowflakes  i think there are some general  trends that you can point at but there's  there's no one single thing in some  cases it could be  that  a person  put so much  into that relationship  that  the very act of grieving it that would  get them past it and allow them to to be  open to something new  is too painful  or it could be that they feel a sense of  shame and that motivates them so much  that they can't look at what's happening  and it's better to feel these other  feelings than to feel shame  some people and i'm not saying this is  the case in this case  might even derive some sort of  satisfaction or  something from being stuck in a  situation like that there are many other  possibilities as well
 

